---
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
---
<Base title="TPM — A Technical Product Manager in your terminal">
  <section style="padding: 3rem 0 2rem;">
    <p><span class="badge">Open source · MIT</span></p>
    <h1 style="font-size: 2.8rem; line-height: 1.15; margin: 0.5rem 0 1rem;">
      A Technical Product Manager, in your terminal.
    </h1>
    <p style="font-size: 1.2rem; color: #4d4d4d; max-width: 40rem;">
      TPM audits your software product from the source code alone — reconstructs what you intended,
      imagines what a user experiences given your code, finds the gaps, and specs the
      highest-leverage fixes. No browser, no scraping, no login to your live site.
    </p>
    <p style="margin-top: 2rem;">
      <a href="https://github.com/sinameraji/tpm" class="button">View on GitHub</a>
      <a href="/docs" class="button secondary" style="margin-left: 0.5rem;">Read the method</a>
    </p>
    <pre style="margin-top: 2rem; max-width: 40rem;"><code>npm install -g tpm

cd your-product-repo
tpm init
tpm audit</code></pre>
    <p style="color: #4d4d4d; font-size: 0.9rem; max-width: 40rem; margin-top: 1rem;">
      Every new device gets one free audit on our hosted Cloudflare Workers AI credits. After that,
      <a href="/self-host">set up your own Cloudflare account</a> for unlimited audits.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section style="padding: 2rem 0; border-top: 1px solid #e4e4e0;">
    <h2 style="margin-bottom: 1.5rem;">How it works — read the code, imagine the UX.</h2>
    <p style="max-width: 40rem;">
      A skilled PM reading your codebase can narrate the user's first run from memory. TPM does the
      same thing, algorithmically. It extracts your routes, forms, components, copy, auth gates
      and tracking events — then reconstructs the intended product from the code, and a plausible
      user journey from the same code.
    </p>
    <p style="max-width: 40rem;">
      This means TPM works on anything you have source access to — enterprise SaaS,
      authenticated-only dashboards, anything behind a paywall — because it never needs to visit
      your live product.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section style="padding: 2rem 0; border-top: 1px solid #e4e4e0;">
    <h2 style="margin-bottom: 1.5rem;">The method is the product.</h2>
    <p style="max-width: 40rem;">
      Every TPM audit runs the same six-stage pipeline. Each stage produces a validated YAML
      artifact.
    </p>
    <ol style="max-width: 40rem; margin-top: 1rem;">
      <li><strong>Intent extraction</strong> — Lean Canvas reconstructed from the codebase.</li>
      <li><strong>Imagined critical path</strong> — user journey inferred from routes, forms, components.</li>
      <li><strong>Delta analysis</strong> — classifies every step and names the intent mismatches.</li>
      <li><strong>Leverage prioritization</strong> — ranks problems with an explicit argument, not a formula.</li>
      <li><strong>Solution specs + prototypes</strong> — top 5 fixes, each with a working HTML prototype.</li>
      <li><strong>Artifact assembly</strong> — spec.md + spec.html, PM-grade.</li>
    </ol>
  </section>

  <section style="padding: 2rem 0; border-top: 1px solid #e4e4e0;">
    <h2>Built for product owners who know what they're looking at.</h2>
    <p style="max-width: 40rem;">
      TPM won't tell you to add confetti animations or rainbow gradients. It finds the step in
      your signup flow that every honest user abandons, the page where your marketing copy
      contradicts the product, and the feature whose code exists but no user can reach it. Then
      it gives you a structured fix with a prototype.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section style="padding: 2rem 0; border-top: 1px solid #e4e4e0;">
    <h2>Open source</h2>
    <p style="max-width: 40rem;">
      All source, all patterns, all prompts: <a href="https://github.com/sinameraji/tpm">github.com/sinameraji/tpm</a>.
      Fork it, audit it, send PRs, or just run it on your own infra.
    </p>
  </section>
</Base>
